    content: `
      <p>New tile flooring transforms the look and feel of any Pakistani home. Whether you are renovating an old house with cracked or dirty mosaic floors, upgrading from basic cement to polished porcelain, or finishing a new construction, tile installation is one of the most impactful and cost-effective home improvements you can make.</p>

      <p>But tiling is also one of the most skill-dependent trades in construction. A beautiful tile poorly laid will crack, lift, or show uneven grout lines within months. An experienced mason laying the same tile will produce a result that lasts 20 or 30 years. This guide explains the entire process so you know what to expect and how to ensure a quality result. For professional help, <a href="/register">book a verified mason through Service Knock</a>.</p>

      <h2>Types of Tiles Available in Pakistan</h2>
      <p>The tile market in Pakistan has grown enormously in recent years, with local manufacturers and Chinese imports offering a wide variety of options at different price points.</p>

      <h3>Ceramic Tiles</h3>
      <p>The most affordable and widely used tile in Pakistani homes. Suitable for interior floors and walls. Not recommended for high-traffic exterior areas as they can be slippery when wet. Price range: Rs. 800–3,000 per box (covering approximately 11–13 square feet).</p>

      <h3>Porcelain Tiles</h3>
      <p>Denser, harder, and more water-resistant than ceramic. Suitable for both indoor and outdoor use, including bathrooms, kitchens, and exterior terraces. More expensive but significantly more durable. Price range: Rs. 2,500–8,000 per box. Brands like Master Tiles, Shabbir Tiles, and RAK (imported) are popular in Pakistan.</p>

      <h3>Marble</h3>
      <p>A traditional choice for Pakistani homes, especially in living rooms and main halls. Natural marble from Balochistan (white and green marble) and imported options are available. Requires sealing and polishing to maintain its appearance. Price range: Rs. 150–600+ per square foot, installed.</p>

      <h3>Granite</h3>
      <p>Extremely hard and durable. Ideal for kitchens, bathrooms, and high-traffic areas. More expensive than marble but virtually scratch-proof. Price range: Rs. 200–800+ per square foot.</p>

      <h2>Floor Preparation — The Most Critical Step</h2>
      <p>This is where most tiling jobs in Pakistan go wrong. Cutting corners on floor preparation leads to tiles cracking, lifting, and grout lines cracking — sometimes within months. A proper mason will never rush this step.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Level assessment:</strong> The floor must be checked for level across the entire area using a spirit level. Depressions or humps must be filled or ground down before tiling begins.</li>
        <li><strong>Surface cleaning:</strong> All dust, oil, and loose material must be removed. Old cement and adhesive patches must be chipped off and the surface roughened for good adhesion.</li>
        <li><strong>Waterproofing:</strong> In bathrooms, kitchens, and any wet area, a waterproof membrane must be applied to the floor and at least 15 cm up all walls before tiles are laid. This is non-negotiable.</li>
        <li><strong>Curing time:</strong> New concrete floors must be at least 28 days old before tiling. Fresh screed or levelling compound must fully cure per the manufacturer's instructions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Installation Process — Step by Step</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Layout planning:</strong> A good mason plans the tile layout before mixing any cement — marking the centre of the room and planning cuts so that cut tiles at the edges are symmetric and no tile is less than half-size at any visible edge.</li>
        <li><strong>Adhesive or cement bed:</strong> Most Pakistani masons use a dry cement-sand mix (typically 1:3 ratio) as the tile bed. Modern projects increasingly use tile adhesive, which gives better bond strength and is recommended for porcelain tiles and any elevated surface.</li>
        <li><strong>Laying:</strong> Each tile is pressed firmly into the bed with a rubber mallet, levelled with spacers for consistent grout lines, and checked for level against adjacent tiles continuously.</li>
        <li><strong>Cutting:</strong> Accurate cuts require an electric tile cutter — not the manual scoring tools many cheap masons use. Jagged cuts are a sign of inadequate equipment.</li>
        <li><strong>Grouting:</strong> Grout is applied after the adhesive/cement has cured (typically 24 hours), pressed firmly into all joints, and excess is wiped off before it hardens.</li>
        <li><strong>Curing and finishing:</strong> The tiled floor should not be walked on for at least 24 hours. Full grouting cure takes 72 hours.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Labour Costs for Tiling in Pakistan — 2026</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Basic floor tiling (ceramic): Rs. 60–100 per square foot labour only</li>
        <li>Porcelain floor tiling: Rs. 80–120 per square foot</li>
        <li>Marble or granite installation: Rs. 100–180 per square foot</li>
        <li>Wall tiling (bathroom): Rs. 80–130 per square foot</li>
        <li>Bathroom complete tiling (floor + walls): Rs. 15,000–40,000 for an average bathroom</li>
        <li>Living room floor (500 sq ft porcelain): Rs. 40,000–70,000 labour only</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These prices are for labour only. Tile cost, adhesive, grout, and other materials are additional. Always get a detailed quote that separates labour and materials. For accurate pricing in your city, <a href="/register">post a request on Service Knock</a>.</p>

      <h2>How Long Does Tiling Take?</h2>
      <p>A skilled mason with one helper can tile approximately 100–150 square feet of floor per day. A standard 3-bedroom house renovation (approximately 2,000 square feet of floor space) takes 15–20 working days. This includes preparation time and rest days for curing. Do not let anyone tell you they will tile your whole house in 3 days — rushed tiling is bad tiling.</p>

      <h2>Choosing the Right Mason</h2>
      <p>Look for a mason who insists on proper floor preparation (this shows professionalism), uses spacers for consistent grout lines, has an electric tile cutter, and can show you previous work. Avoid masons who promise very fast completion or give quotes significantly below market rate — both are red flags for shortcuts and poor quality.</p>
      <p><a href="/register">Book a CNIC-verified mason through Service Knock</a> to ensure accountability and quality. For other home improvement topics, read our guides on <a href="/blog/choosing-right-paint-pakistan-climate">choosing the right paint</a> and <a href="/blog/monsoon-home-maintenance-checklist-pakistan">monsoon home maintenance</a>.</p>
    `,
